# servicer_2t.k26asm - a two-tonne servicing vehicle that spends what
# it thrusts and carries a standard docking interface.
#
# WHAT THIS IS. A modelled article. Nothing in this file is cited and
# nothing here is a claim about any vehicle: the dry mass, the tank,
# the hull, the thruster layout, the specific impulse and the wheels
# are all chosen, and each is disclosed as chosen on its own
# provenance line. What the article is for is a servicing approach to
# an object that does not help, which needs a craft that can find an
# arbitrary bearing, turn to face it, and run out of propellant if it
# wastes any.
#
# WHY NOT THE ARTICLES BESIDE IT. `crew_vehicle_10t.k26asm` carries
# mass and inertia from a published standard and its hull was solved
# to reproduce them, so it cannot be given a tank without moving the
# derived tensor off the figures it reproduces.
# `servicing_vehicle.k26asm` has the tank but no docking interface,
# and it is a ten-tonne article whose transverse moment is 42 000
# kg m^2. That matters here in a way it does not there: this task
# starts with the target's interface pointing in a direction the
# episode draws, so the craft has to slew through as much as half a
# turn before it can dock, and at 5 N m against 42 000 kg m^2 a half
# turn takes about five minutes and saturates the wheels doing it. A
# servicer is a small vehicle in reality and this article is sized
# accordingly.
#
# THE HULL AND THE MASS. A box 2.6 m along the docking axis and 1.9 m
# across, at 1985 kg dry, with a 15 kg manoeuvre allocation making two
# tonnes. The box is chosen for what it is: a bus, not a capsule. Its
# transverse moment about the centre of mass comes out at 1723 kg m^2
# under this compiler's closed forms and its moment about the docking
# axis at 1198, both measured back out of a compiled world by
# commanding a known wheel torque and reading the angular
# acceleration it produced. The transverse figure is what the wheel
# sizing below is taken against.
#
# THE TANK, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. Fifteen kilogrammes on a two-tonne
# vehicle, which is three parts in four hundred. That is not a mission
# load and is not offered as one; it is the allocation for one close
# approach, and the article is written that way on purpose. At the
# specific impulse below the whole load is worth
# 300 * 9.80665 * ln(2000 / 1985), which is 22.1 m/s of velocity
# change. What that buys against what this approach costs is the
# environment's business and is measured there rather than asserted
# here.
#
# The tank sits 0.30 m aft of the hull's centre, so the vehicle's
# centre of mass travels as it empties: at a full tank it is
# 0.30 * 15 / 2000 aft of the hull's centre, which is 2.25 mm, and at
# an empty one it is at the hull's centre. Thruster torques are taken
# about the centre of mass and travel with it, and the consequence is
# worth stating exactly because it is a load on the wheels rather
# than a curiosity. The four thrusters along the docking axis are
# unaffected, since a force along that axis about a centre displaced
# along the same axis has the same moment arm either way. The eight
# lateral thrusters are not: each opposed pair is separated along the
# axis, so a pair that is torque-free on an empty tank leaves a
# residual couple of 0.0045 N m per newton on a full one, which is
# 0.18 N m with the pair at full throttle against 1.0 N m of wheel.
# A pair at the tenth of a throttle a terminal approach uses leaves
# 0.018 N m, and 200 s of it is 3.6 N m s against a wheel that holds
# 80.
#
# The offset is deliberately small for that reason and deliberately
# not zero: a centred tank would have hidden the effect entirely
# rather than shown it small, and an offset large enough to fight the
# wheels would have made this article's attitude control a study of
# its own tank rather than of the task.
#
# The tank is a box of half extent 0.116 m, which is 0.0125 cubic
# metres and puts fifteen kilogrammes at about 1201 kg per cubic
# metre, the order of a storable bipropellant. Its placement and its
# shape are an arrangement rather than a measurement.
#
# THE THRUSTERS. Twelve, at 40 N each, in the two-ring opposed layout
# the articles beside this one use: two pushing the vehicle toward its
# interface, two pushing it away, and eight lateral in four groups of
# two, one forward and one aft of the centre of mass on each side, so
# that a pair translates and either one alone turns. An opposed pair
# gives this vehicle 0.04 m/s^2, and the whole set at full throttle
# spends 0.163 kg/s and empties the tank in 92 s.
#
# The specific impulse is 300 s on every thruster, which is the right
# order for a storable bipropellant and is not read from a source.
# Every thruster carries the same figure because this article has one
# kind of thruster, and a spread would make the published delta-v
# channel depend on which thruster the vehicle last had propellant for
# rather than on the budget it has.
#
# THE WHEELS, AND WHY THEY ARE SIZED TO A SLEW. Three, at 1.0 N m and
# 80 N m s. The torque is the one that turns this article through half
# a turn in about a hundred seconds: a bang-bang slew of pi radians at
# an angular acceleration of 1.0 / 1723 covers it in
# 2 * sqrt((pi / 2) / (1.0 / 1723)), which is 104 s, and the momentum
# that slew peaks at is 1723 * 0.0302, which is 52 N m s, so the wheel
# does not saturate performing the manoeuvre it is sized for. The
# momentum limit is that peak with about half again in hand. Both
# figures are chosen against this task's own requirement rather than
# read from a source, and a wheel of this torque is a large one.
#
# The saturation rate follows the convention the articles beside this
# one use: a spin inertia of 0.08 kg m^2 against 80 N m s saturates at
# 1000 rad/s.
#
# THE INTERFACE. One port on the first body axis, 1.35 m ahead of the
# hull's centre and therefore 0.05 m ahead of its forward face, so it
# is the first geometry another craft meets. Its capture envelope is
# `idss_e`, which this compiler carries from the International Docking
# System Standard Interface Definition Document, Revision E, October
# 2016. The envelope is the standard's; where the interface sits is
# this article's choice.
#
# The port arm is 1.35 m, so a pitch or yaw error swings the mating
# plane sideways by 1.35 times the angle. The envelope allows 0.10 m
# of lateral misalignment and 4.0 degrees of pitch or yaw, and 4.0
# degrees on this arm is 0.094 m, so on this article the two limits
# very nearly coincide: an attitude error at the angular limit spends
# almost exactly the whole lateral allowance. That is a property of
# the arm and it is stated because it tells a reader which residual
# binds first, which on a longer-armed vehicle would be a different
# one.

assembly servicer_2t
    frame x_to_port

    provenance mass "chosen: a 1985 kg dry hull and a 15 kg manoeuvre allocation, making a two-tonne servicer; no vehicle is claimed and no figure is cited" modelled
    provenance hull_geometry "chosen: a box 2.6 m along the docking axis and 1.9 m across, sized as a small servicing bus rather than solved for any figure" modelled
    provenance inertia "computed: this compiler's closed forms over the declared components at the declared fill" computed
    provenance tank "a chosen tank placement, held 0.30 m aft of the hull's centre so the centre of mass travels as it empties without loading the wheels; this is an arrangement and not a measurement" modelled
    provenance isp "a chosen figure of the right order for a storable bipropellant thruster; no source is cited because no article is claimed" modelled
    provenance thrust "chosen: 40 N a thruster, giving an opposed pair 0.04 m/s^2 on this article, which closes 200 m and stops inside a few minutes without making a control step of one second too coarse to hold a capture band 0.05 m/s wide" modelled
    provenance thruster_layout "two rings at the hull ends in opposed pairs, chosen and not derived; no thruster position is published for any vehicle this article stands in for" modelled
    provenance wheel_limits "chosen against this task's own slew: 1.0 N m turns this article's 1723 kg m^2 transverse moment through half a turn in 104 s bang-bang, peaking at 52 N m s, and the momentum limit is that peak with half again in hand" modelled
    provenance wheel_friction "a chosen friction model of the right order for a wheel of this size; no figure for the viscous, Coulomb or dead-rate terms is published or derived here" modelled
    provenance port_forward "the mating plane 0.05 m ahead of the hull's forward face so it is the first geometry another craft meets; the standard publishes the offset only in a figure" modelled
    provenance capture_envelope "International Docking System Standard Interface Definition Document, Revision E, October 2016: the envelope this compiler carries as idss_e" cited

    component hull
        mass 1985.0
        at 0.0 0.0 0.0
        collider box 1.3 0.95 0.95
    end

    component tank
        mass 15.0
        at -0.30 0.0 0.0
        collider box 0.116 0.116 0.116
        propellant
    end

    port forward
        at 1.35 0.0 0.0
        axis 1.0 0.0 0.0
        roll_ref 0.0 1.0 0.0
        capture idss_e
    end

    # Two thrusters push the vehicle toward its docking interface and
    # two push it away. Each pair sits on opposite sides of the hull
    # at one ring, so firing a pair together is a force through the
    # centre of mass and firing one alone is a pitch or yaw command.
    thruster close_yp
        at -1.3 0.95 0.0
        dir 1.0 0.0 0.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster close_ym
        at -1.3 -0.95 0.0
        dir 1.0 0.0 0.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster brake_yp
        at 1.3 0.95 0.0
        dir -1.0 0.0 0.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster brake_ym
        at 1.3 -0.95 0.0
        dir -1.0 0.0 0.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end

    # Eight lateral thrusters, four to a plane. Each group of two is
    # one forward and one aft of the centre of mass with the same
    # direction, so the pair translates and either one alone turns.
    thruster lat_yp_fwd
        at 1.3 -0.95 0.0
        dir 0.0 1.0 0.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_yp_aft
        at -1.3 -0.95 0.0
        dir 0.0 1.0 0.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_ym_fwd
        at 1.3 0.95 0.0
        dir 0.0 -1.0 0.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_ym_aft
        at -1.3 0.95 0.0
        dir 0.0 -1.0 0.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_zp_fwd
        at 1.3 0.0 -0.95
        dir 0.0 0.0 1.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_zp_aft
        at -1.3 0.0 -0.95
        dir 0.0 0.0 1.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_zm_fwd
        at 1.3 0.0 0.95
        dir 0.0 0.0 -1.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end
    thruster lat_zm_aft
        at -1.3 0.0 0.95
        dir 0.0 0.0 -1.0
        thrust 40.0
        isp_s 300.0
    end

    # Three reaction wheels, which spend nothing. A craft that turns
    # to face an interface on its wheels rather than on opposed
    # thrusters keeps its propellant for the approach, and this task
    # makes that a decision worth taking rather than a preference.
    wheel wheel_x
        axis 1.0 0.0 0.0
        spin_inertia 0.08
        max_momentum 80.0
        max_torque 1.0
        viscous 1.0e-5
        coulomb 2.0e-4
        dead_rate 1.0e-3
    end
    wheel wheel_y
        axis 0.0 1.0 0.0
        spin_inertia 0.08
        max_momentum 80.0
        max_torque 1.0
        viscous 1.0e-5
        coulomb 2.0e-4
        dead_rate 1.0e-3
    end
    wheel wheel_z
        axis 0.0 0.0 1.0
        spin_inertia 0.08
        max_momentum 80.0
        max_torque 1.0
        viscous 1.0e-5
        coulomb 2.0e-4
        dead_rate 1.0e-3
    end
end
